benchmarks/subset/ulr_vs_pulr -- the 360 programs of [1] ULR-Baseline vs P-ULR for --subset
============================================================================================

The programs on which scripts/run_full_evaluation.sh --subset runs [1], with a 300 s timeout per
Ultimate run. The files are unchanged copies of programs of benchmarks/ulr_vs_pulr (Table 1, Figure 5).

How they were chosen (benchmarks/subset/select_subsets.py, from our logs in logs/): 200 C and 160
Boogie programs, the proportions of the benchmark, among the 2679 whose extraction took at most
200 s in our run, so that the 300 s timeout leaves their lasso traces unchanged. The draws
use random.Random(seed) for seed = 1, 2, ...; the first one whose traces reproduce the whole
benchmark within the bounds below is kept: seed 347. A uniform draw does not, since a few traces
carry most of the time differences; the bound on the largest single trace keeps the subset's
ratios from resting on one trace.

                                           subset  benchmark   bounds
    terminating, ULR-Baseline / P-ULR-Seq      12.1x      11.5x   [9.8x, 13.2x]
    terminating, ULR-Baseline / P-ULR-Par7      27.8x      25.1x   [21.3x, 28.8x]
    non-term., ULR-Baseline / P-ULR-Seq       6.9x       6.4x   [5.1x, 7.7x]
    non-term., ULR-Baseline / P-ULR-Par7       6.4x       6.0x   [4.8x, 7.2x]
    GNTA share (non-terminating)            12.7%      10.9%   [5.9%, 15.9%]
    non-affine ranking functions               10        844   [3, -]
    GNTA traces                                28        173   [3, -]
    largest trace, of a terminating total      13.4%      16.9%   [0.0%, 15.0%]
    largest trace, of a non-term. total       3.8%       0.8%   [0.0%, 25.0%]
    traces every tool proved                  469       5727   [100, -]
    running time (extraction, P-ULR)         1.3h

The subset keeps the speed-ups of Table 1, not two shares of the benchmark: its terminating traces
are 96 % affine (80 % in the benchmark), and 53 % of its traces terminate (72 %). Both shares come
from a handful of programs with hundreds of traces each -- polyrank4.t2.c alone dumps 765 of the
benchmark's 844 non-affine ones -- whose extraction takes up to 3,000 s, beyond any subset.

The columns give our result for each program, from the run of logs/ULR_vs_PULR_logs.zip
(tools/UAutomizer-linux, timeout 3,000 s): the verdict Ultimate reached, the time it took (from
the timestamps of its log) and the number of lasso traces it dumped. ERROR means that Ultimate
stopped on an exception, with the traces dumped until then.

